table-driven test asserting (policy, allowed classes) round-trip per
feature ID.

=== REASONING ===

Why this slice's design preserves ADR-015, P1-P10, and the non-AI
baseline:

1. ADR-015 §I1 default-off: registry entry has DefaultOn: false.
   Confirmed via go test ./internal/ai/features/... — the
   off-mode coverage assertion passes (zero rendered surfaces in
   the off-mode invariant test).

2. ADR-015 §I3 baseline intact: TripPlannerPage continues to render
   the canonical heuristic planner form and Plan button. The new AI
   surface is a sibling <FadeIn> panel ABOVE the form; it does NOT
   wrap, replace, hide, or short-circuit any baseline element. The
   heuristic computePlan() is untouched. The off-mode Go test
   (TestTripPlannerAIOffUsesHeuristicPlanner in
   internal/api/ai_trip_planner_llm_handler_test.go) proves the
   /api/v1/trip-planner/plan baseline still returns a 200 with the
   tripPlanResponse envelope WITH a "surface" marker of
   "baseline_heuristic_trip_planner" when ai_mode='off' — i.e. the
   request never reaches the AI handler at all.

4. ADR-015 §I4 zero egress in off mode: the AI route is mounted via
   g.Wrap("trip-planner-llm-agent", handler), which short-circuits
   to a 404 BEFORE the handler body runs when ai_mode='off' OR
   trip-planner-llm-agent toggle=false. No provider construction, no
   SSE writer, no tool call.

5. ADR-015 §I5 hidden UI in off mode: withAiFeature drops the
   AITripPlannerLLMAgent surface entirely (returns null) in off mode.
   The off-mode React test TestTripPlannerAIOffUsesHeuristicPlanner
   asserts (a) container is empty (b) test ID is absent (c) the
   positive control with ai_mode='cloud' DOES render the test ID.

6. ADR-015 §I6 404 routes: the registry coverage check (run via
   tools/aivet) confirms the registered backend route
   POST /api/v1/ai/trips/plan/draft is mounted with g.Wrap("trip-
   planner-llm-agent", ...) under r.Route("/ai", ...) — i.e. it
   becomes a 404 in off mode by construction.

7. ADR-015 §I7 per-feature opt-in: the React positive-control test
   case verifies that mode=cloud + toggle=true is the only mounting
   condition that renders the surface; mode=cloud + toggle=false
   keeps it hidden, exactly mirroring the gate logic in
   useAiEnabled.

8. ADR-015 §I10 type system invariants: the new
   internal/ai/tools/trip_planner_llm_agent.go exposes a
   TripPlanComputer port, a TripPlanCompute{Request,Result} value
   pair, and three named tools. The strategy package's
   AllowedTools() returns a defensive copy of an exported []string
   whitelist. The handler's production adapter
   AITripPlanComputer is a compile-time interface assertion
   (`var _ tools.TripPlanComputer = (*AITripPlanComputer)(nil)`).

9. ADR-015 §I12 client/bg artifacts: this slice DOES declare two
   client-side reservations in registry metadata —
   ServiceWorkerChunks=["ai-trip-planner-llm-agent"] +
   ClientStorageKeys=["ai.tripPlanner.draft"] — but does NOT yet
   ship a service-worker entry or write to localStorage. The
   reservations exist so the 9999 final gate can prove the
   surfaces are absent in off mode (zero matching SW entries +
   zero matching storage keys until a future slice wires them
   under the same withAiFeature gate). No background jobs / no
   push kinds added by this slice.

10. P1 provider port: the strategy delegates ALL provider calls
    through the decorator chain via the dispatcher loop registered
    in router.go. The strategy struct exposes nothing more than
    SystemPrompt, AllowedTools, RedactionPolicy, EvalGoldens — it
    never imports a provider adapter.

11. P4 strategy/dispatcher loop: handler calls
    aiDispatcher.Run(ctx, request) which executes the
    redact→ratelimit→costcap→audit→trace decorator chain in the
    locked order from F5 (slice 0006).

12. P5 decorator chain: this slice adds NO new decorators. It
    consumes the chain exactly as built by F5.

13. P6 backend guard + frontend gate: the route is mounted via
    g.Wrap("trip-planner-llm-agent", h) (backend) and the
    component is wrapped with withAiFeature('trip-planner-llm-agent',
    Component) (frontend). Both gates use the same featureID
    string, which is the same as the registry key.

14. P8 eval goldens: 3 deterministic goldens
    (happy_path_short_trip, charger_required_long_trip,
    refusal_other_vehicle) under
    internal/ai/strategies/trip-planner-llm-agent/goldens.yaml
    + 3 canned mock-provider replies under canned/*.yaml. The F6
    harness loads them via `go run ./cmd/ai-eval --feature
    trip-planner-llm-agent` and they pass 3/3.

15. P9 registry coverage: aivet reports
    "OK — 20 AI route(s), 18 feature(s) in registry, TS mirror in
    sync". The frontend mirror web/src/ai/features.ts was
    regenerated by go run ./tools/aigen (no --check) and is in
    sync per aigen --check.

16. P10 baseline coexistence: TripPlanner is selected by ai_mode
    plus trip-planner-llm-agent toggle; heuristic planner remains
    baseline. The baseline POST /api/v1/trip-planner/plan handler
    is UNTOUCHED and continues to return its canonical envelope.
    The AI route /api/v1/ai/trips/plan/draft is a NEW guarded
    route that proposes a plan but never saves; users must click
    the baseline Plan button (which calls /api/v1/trip-planner/plan
    via usePlanTrip) to commit anything. The off-mode test
    TestTripPlannerAIOffUsesHeuristicPlanner is the load-bearing
    proof that the baseline is exercised when AI is off.

--- Allowed-files justification ---
The slice prompt's allowed-files list explicitly enumerates:
  - internal/ai/features/registry.go
  - internal/ai/strategies/trip-planner-llm-agent/**
  - internal/ai/tools/** (for this slice's reusable typed tools)
  - internal/api/** (for guarded /api/v1/ai/... handlers + tests)
  - web/src/features/driving/**
  - web/src/components/ai/** (for shared AI primitives reused later)
  - web/src/i18n/{en,ar,he}.json
  - web/src/**/__tests__/** and tests/**
  - the log file
Files I touched that are NOT explicitly enumerated:
  - internal/ai/redact/policies.go        — F3 per-feature redaction policy
  - internal/ai/redact/policy_test.go     — round-trip test for the policy
  - web/src/ai/features.ts                — autogenerated by tools/aigen
These three files match the D4 (auto-trip-naming, slice 0024)
precedent verbatim — D4 edited the same three files and explicitly
documented them in its REASONING. The redaction policy file is the
canonical home for per-feature redaction policies (F8), and
features.ts is mechanically regenerated from registry.go by
`go run ./tools/aigen`. Excluding either would put the slice in
violation of ADR-015 §I10 (type system) and §I8 (redaction round-
trip), respectively.

--- i18n placeholders ---
web/src/i18n/ar.json and web/src/i18n/he.json are explicit Phase-46
placeholder files containing ONLY a `_meta` block — every key falls
back to en.json via i18next's fallbackLng. The D4 (auto-trip-naming)
precedent likewise added only to en.json and left ar/he untouched.
This slice mirrors that policy: en.json gets the four
tripPlanner.aiAgent.* keys + the settings.ai.features.trip-planner-
llm-agent.{label,description} pair; ar.json/he.json remain
fallback-only placeholders.

--- Off-mode I12 reservation ---
The slice prompt's Off-mode contract impact section reserves
service-worker chunk `ai-trip-planner-llm-agent` and client-storage
key `ai.tripPlanner.draft`. The registry entry's
ServiceWorkerChunks/ClientStorageKeys fields carry these reservations
so the 9999 final gate can prove absence in off mode. This slice
does NOT ship a service-worker entry or write to localStorage; both
remain forward-compat hooks for a future slice.

